    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is a clean, additive mirror of the existing \"ensure\" rule pattern in this codebase.\n\n- **Logic is in the right unit.** `EnsureCityCapacity` is a single-method function class wired as its own pipeline step between `ensureUniqueHousehold` and `build` \u2014 exactly where a pre-build validation guard belongs, alongside siblings `EnsureUniqueHousehold` and `EnsureUniqueOwnerTelephone`. It operates on the `@Val OwnerFieldsDto` request before the owner is built/saved, consistent with the stated \"count over existing owners\" intent.\n- **Follows the established escalation convention precisely.** `CityAtCapacityException` (checked, message-building constructor) mirrors `DuplicateHouseholdException`; `CityAtCapacityExceptionHandler` is structurally identical to `DuplicateHouseholdExceptionHandler` (same `ProblemDetails.build(...)` + `ResponseEntity.status(CONFLICT)` shape); and the `officefloor/escalation/...yml` is registered the same way. Nothing becomes a catch-all.\n- **No inappropriate duplication.** The city-matching stream filter overlaps conceptually with `AssignCustomerCode`'s per-city count, but each is a one-line predicate used in two places with different intent (capacity gate vs. sequence number); extracting a shared helper for that would be premature, and the javadoc already cross-references the matching approach. There's no established `Cities` helper analogous to `Households` to slot into, so inlining is the idiomatic choice here.\n- Naming (`EnsureCityCapacity`, `CITY_CAPACITY`), transaction governance on the new step, and the `next:` rewiring all match the surrounding file.\n\nA maintainer of this repo would find this natural and consistent with the existing owner-creation pipeline.",
